"html": "    <div class=\"home-emblem\"><svg class=\"il\"><use href=\"#il-collar\"/></svg></div>\n    <p class=\"lead\">BDSM is not \"violent sex\" and not a \"deviation\". It is a consensual game of power, sensation and trust between adults who chose it, where the boundaries are clearer than in any \"regular\" sex, and precisely because of that you can let go completely. The acronym covers three overlapping worlds, and you can love one of them without touching the others.</p>\n\n    <h2>The three worlds</h2>\n    <div class=\"grid2\">\n      <div class=\"term\"><b>B/D · Bondage and Discipline</b>Scarves, ropes, cuffs, waiting positions, rules for the evening and penalties for breaking them. The point is not pain but the absence of choice: when you cannot move, the body stops managing and starts feeling.</div>\n      <div class=\"term\"><b>D/s · Dominance and submission</b>The power exchange itself: who decides, who obeys, who leads the evening. It can be as subtle as a look and one command, and it can fill the whole night. The submissive side is not \"weak\"; they are the one who gives the power, and can take it back with a word.</div>\n      <div class=\"term\"><b>S/M · Sensation and intensity</b>Sadism and Masochism. Slaps, strikes, pinches, cold and heat, clamps, wax. The body turns controlled pain into adrenaline and endorphins, and inside arousal it is felt as intensity, not injury. For most couples it is far more \"strong and pleasant\" than \"painful\".</div>\n      <div class=\"term\"><b>What they all share</b>Consent in advance, the ability to stop at any moment, and an ending that brings you both back to yourselves. Without these three it is not BDSM, it is just violence or just bad sex.</div>\n    </div>\n\n    <h2>Why it works</h2>\n    <p>A power exchange lets each of you set down, for a moment, who you are during the day. Whoever manages, decides and is responsible all day can get one evening where someone else decides for them, and the mind goes quiet. Whoever usually pleases and accommodates can get permission to be demanding, sharp and unapologetic. The combination of sexual arousal with tension, anticipation and hormonal release creates states of mind called <b>subspace</b> (floating, flooding, blurred time in the submissive) and <b>topspace</b> (sharp focus and full presence in the Dom). And after the evening, sometimes a day or two later, <b>drop</b> can arrive: low mood, tiredness, sensitivity. That is chemistry, not a sign something was wrong, and it is exactly what \"After\" is meant to soften.</p>\n    <div class=\"pull\">The real power in the room always belongs to the one who can say the stop word. The Dom only borrows it, for a few hours.</div>\n\n    <h2>Roles</h2>\n    <p><b>Dominant, Top, Dom</b>: the one who decides, directs, gives the sensation. In the app: \"Dom\", {D} in the texts. <b>Submissive, bottom, sub</b>: the one who receives, obeys, experiences. In the app: \"sub\". <b>Switch</b>: someone who enjoys both sides, sometimes in the same evening. That is why there is a \"switches every task\" mode and a \"draw\" mode here: for most couples the best dynamic is the one that is not fixed forever.</p>\n\n    <h2>Consent: the shared language</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>SSC</b> (Safe, Sane, Consensual): the classic formula.</li>\n      <li><b>RACK</b> (Risk-Aware Consensual Kink): you are both aware of the specific risk and choose it with open eyes. A more precise formula for an experienced couple: not \"no risk\" but \"known and agreed risk\".</li>\n      <li><b>Negotiation</b>: what is a yes, what is a maybe, what is never, and up to which intensity. That is the \"Limits and intensity\" screen here: each marks alone, and only what you are both open to enters play.</li>\n      <li><b>Stop word</b>: a word you would not say by accident. \"Red\" stops everything at once, \"yellow\" slows down and checks. When the mouth is busy or the hands are tied: an agreed signal, for example three taps. Consent is not a signature at the start of the evening, it is an ongoing state that can be withdrawn any second.</li>\n      <li><b>After</b> (Aftercare): water, a blanket, a hug, talking or not talking. Coming down is part of the scene, not something you do if there is time. That is why every evening here ends with an \"After\" card.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h2>The arc of a scene</h2>\n    <p>A good scene is built like a story: an <b>opening</b> that moves you from everyday life into the roles (a collar closing, a first command, slow undressing), a <b>build</b> that raises sensation and tension gradually, a <b>peak</b> where body and mind are fully released, and a <b>closing</b> that brings you back. That is why the evening's deck here warms up slowly: four tasks at intensity 1, four at intensity 2, four at intensity 3, and always \"After\" at the end. Jumping straight to intensity 3 is like opening a film at its climax: it works less, not more.</p>\n\n    <h2>The categories here, and what is behind them</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>Control and orders</b>: the basis of D/s. Commands, titles, rules, waiting positions. The power is in the tone, not the volume.</li>\n      <li><b>Senses</b>: blindfold, ice, feather, wax, clamps. When one eye closes, all the others sharpen.</li>\n      <li><b>Bondage</b>: restraint that exposes. Golden rule: two fingers fit under every knot, and never on the joint itself or on the neck.</li>\n      <li><b>Impact</b>: buttocks and the back of the thighs are the place. Start light, build slowly, stroke between rounds. Never on the kidneys, spine or joints.</li>\n      <li><b>Denial and orgasm control</b>: edging, orgasm denial, control over release. The greatest power in the room is saying \"not yet\".</li>\n      <li><b>Role play</b>: strangers, boss, examination, object. A way to say things that are hard to say in the first person.</li>\n      <li><b>Mouth and tongue, toys, words, exhibition, anal, feet</b>: each a subject of its own, which gets a completely different flavor with a little control.</li>\n      <li><b>Advanced</b>: hand on the throat (a gesture only, no pressure that interferes with breathing or blood flow), fluids and cum play. These categories follow the same rules as all the others: a \"no\" from one of you removes them, a \"maybe\" brings them in. You do not both need to be an enthusiastic \"yes\", it is enough that you are both open.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h2>What makes a long-time couple good at this</h2>\n    <p>Experience helps, but what really makes the difference is three habits. <b>Talk the morning after</b>: what was amazing, what was less so, what surprised you. Without defensiveness. <b>Switch sometimes</b>: even someone sure they are \"only a Dom\" discovers things when they receive for one evening. <b>Treat the stop word as a success</b>: when it is said, someone trusted enough to use it, and that is what makes it possible to go further next time.</p>\n    <div class=\"pull\">The limits are not the fence of the game. They are the field. Without a field there is no game.</div>\n\n    <h2>Where the name comes from</h2>\n    <p>Ktifa is Hebrew for velvet: soft to the touch, deep in color, hiding something strong underneath. Like a good evening of control.</p>\n"
